1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 {All this is well-known
under the name ''The Dragon Variation''. It has been normal in this variation
for Black's QN to be developed early at c6. Of recent years, however, a
different plan has been gaining favour: following Reshevsky's pattern, Black
defers committing himself for the time being on the positioning of this knight.
} 6. Be3 Bg7 7. f3 {The suystem named after the soviet master Rowzer,
introduced a quarter of a century ago and generally regarded ever since as the
best.} a6 8. Bc4 b5 {Still following Reshevsky's recipe: it is to the
development of his QB to which Black gives priority.} 9. Bb3 Bb7 10. Qd2 Nbd7
11. O-O-O Nc5 {White's KB is sufficiently active to justify the time spent on
removing it in exchange for the knight.} 12. Kb1 {White intends to exchange
off the black square bishops. He clears the king from c1 to clear that square
if necessary for a rook, to operate along the c-file.} Nxb3 13. cxb3 $1 O-O 14.
Bh6 Bxh6 15. Qxh6 b4 $1 ({The only move. Anything else allows effective
counter­play, e.g.} 15... e5 16. Nc2 $1 {followed by Ne3 and White dominates
d5.}) 16. e5 ({Littlewood embarks on an impetuous and deeply calculated attack
- deeply but not precisely calculated. A quieter continuation such as} 16. Nce2
e5 17. Nc2 a5 {might have caused Black more trouble}) ({On the other hand} 16.
Nd5 Bxd5 ({This is an improvement on the continuation considered in
light-hearted analysis among the other players,} 16... Nxd5 17. exd5 Bxd5 18.
Nf5 gxf5 19. Rxd5 e6 20. Rxd6 Qxd6 21. Qg5+ {, White getting perpetual check 
(BH Wood)}) 17. exd5 Qd7 {followed by ...e5 would have been in Black's favour.
(This is an improvement considered in light-hearted analysis among the other
players,}) 16... Nd7 ({Of course not} 16... bxc3 17. exf6 exf6 18. bxc3 {
with a clear positional advantage to White.}) 17. h4 ({Although I had foreseen
this move, when it was actually made I realised that with such a dangerous
opponent I must of course probe thoroughly into all the attacking
possibilities of the situation before making another move. So I devoted some
time on this position and satisfied myself that Littlewood had, unfortunately
for himself, over-looked one little refinement The continuation} 17. exd6 {
would be inadequate because of} e5 $1 (17... bxc3 {is also playable:} 18. Nf5
c2+ 19. Kxc2 gxf5 20. Qg5+ Kh8 21. dxe7 Qc7+ $1 22. Kb1 Rg8) 18. Nf5) 17...
bxc3 18. h5 dxe5 $1 ({The plausible alternative defence} 18... c2+ 19. Nxc2 g5
{would be quite incorrect:} 20. Qxg5+ Kh8 21. exd6 {secures White plentiful
compensation in pawns for the bishop he has given up.}) 19. hxg6 ({After} 19.
bxc3 exd4 20. Rxd4 Qa5 {Black's advantage would be obvious.}) 19... Nf6 20.
bxc3 ({Now everything is clear, as} 20. Nf5 {(or 20 Ne6) would fail against}
c2+ {(White had overlooked this crafty check when he played 15 e5.)} 21. Kxc2
Qc8+ {; White's knight goes next move, all the mating threats disappear and
the game is over.}) (20. gxh7+ Kh8 21. Ne2 c2+ 22. Kxc2 Qc8+ 23. Nc3 Qf5+ 24.
Kc1 Rfd8 $19) 20... exd4 21. gxh7+ (21. Rxd4 Qa5 22. gxh7+ Kh8 23. Kb2 Rfc8 $17
) 21... Kh8 22. Rxd4 Qa5 23. Qe3 Nd5 24. Qd2 Nxc3+ 25. Ka1 Rad8 26. Rc1 Qxa2+ {
To be two pieces up offer a generous variety of winning methods!} 27. Qxa2 Nxa2
28. Rxd8 Rxd8 {Source: CHESS, Vol.27, no.401-2, p129 with original annotations
by Botvinnik} 0-1

