{This was certainly the most exciting game of the event. White's 12th move is
a dubious sacrifice and Barden knew the refutation to past the twentieth move
but Littlewood made things very difficult for him and finally Barden's
habitual time-trouble proved the decisive factor.} 1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4
cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 d6 6. Bg5 e6 7. Qd2 a6 8. O-O-O Bd7 9. f4 Be7 10. Nf3 (
10. Qe1 {is an alternative way to threaten 11 e5.}) 10... b5 11. e5 {This
tempting advance is not quite good enough. Sounder is 11 Bd3.} b4 12. exd6 ({
The obvious} 12. exf6 {is favourable to Black after} bxc3 13. Qxc3 gxf6) 12...
bxc3 13. Qxc3 Bf8 14. f5 Qa5 15. fxe6 fxe6 16. Bxf6 gxf6 17. Qxf6 {All post
mortems showed Black as standing better here. (CHESS)} Rg8 18. Bc4 Bg7 19. Qh4
{This keeps the black knight defending e7.} Rb8 20. Bb3 Rxb3 ({Stockfish 15:} 
20... Qf5 $17) 21. axb3 Qa1+ 22. Kd2 Qxb2 23. Qxh7 Rf8 (23... Bc3+ 24. Ke3 Rxg2
$11) 24. Rhe1 (24. Qh5+ Kd8 25. Qh4+ Ke8 26. Qh5+ Rf7 27. Qc5 Rf5 28. Qc4 Ne5
29. Nxe5 Bh6+ {0-1, Larsen-Panno, Mar del Plata 1958.}) (24. Ke2 Ne5 25. Nd4
Bb5+ 26. Nxb5 axb5 27. Rhf1 {Holaszek,H-Minaya Molano,J World Junior
Championship 1961 (played four months previous to the current game; ½-½, 39
moves)}) 24... Bc3+ 25. Kd3 ({Littlewood had originally intended} 25. Ke2 {
; it is better but} Bxe1 26. Rxe1 Kd8 {still favours Black. "Barden had
already become horribly short of time—3 minutes for 16 moves. Littlewood had
over half an hour yet played his remaining moves as quickly as Barden." (CHESS)
}) 25... Nb4+ 26. Kc4 Bg7 ({Stockfish 15:} 26... Nc6 $1 $17) 27. Nd4 $5 (27.
Qh5+ Kd8 (27... Rf7 28. Ne5 Bxe5 29. Qxe5 $18) 28. Qa5+ Ke8 $11) 27... Nd5 (
27... Nxc2 28. Qg6+ Rf7 29. Rxe6+ $1 Kf8 30. Re7 $18) ({Stockfish 15:} 27...
Na2 $1 $19) 28. Rxe6+ $2 ({After the game Littlewood suggested} 28. Qg6+ Kd8
29. Nxe6+ {when White seems to stand better.}) 28... Bxe6 29. Nxe6 Ne3+ ({
"Cafferty suggested} 29... Nb6+ {but 30 Kd3 saves White." (CHESS) However,
engines support Cafferty's argument:} 30. Kd3 Qc3+ 31. Ke2 Qe5+ 32. Kd3 Qxd6+
33. Ke3 Qxe6+ 34. Qe4 Be5 $19) 30. Kc5 Qc3+ $2 ({With less than a minute on
his clock to reach the 40th move Barden makes the decisive mistake. Black
could have won easily with} 30... Qe5+ 31. Kb6 Qxe6 32. Qxg7 Nxd1 $19) 31. Kb6
Nxd1 (31... Qb4+ 32. Ka7 Nxd1 33. Nxg7+ Kd7 34. Ne8+ Ke6 35. Qe7+ Kd5 36. Qxf8
Qc5+ 37. Kxa6 Qc8+ 38. Ka7 Qxc2 39. Qf3+ Kc5 $18) 32. Nxg7+ $1 Kd7 33. Ne8+ $1
Ke6 (33... Kd8 34. Qe7+ Kc8 35. d7+ {wins}) 34. Qe7+ Kd5 35. Nc7+ (35. d7 {
also wins.}) 35... Kd4 36. Qg7+ Ke4 37. Qxf8 Qb4+ 38. Ka7 Qc5+ 39. Kb7 {
Alexander wrote of this game ''A bad game but much more amusing than most good
ones.'' Botvinnik remarked: ''A real English-style game.'' L. Lindheimer's
observation is also worth quoting: ''Littlewood plays the best bad games I
have ever seen.'' Sources: Sunday Times Magazine, 7 January 1962, p38; CHESS,
Vol.27, no.401-2, January 1962, p134; John Littlewood collection (Keith Brown)}
(39. Kb8 {is the final move given previously. Corrected 22.01.2023.}) ({For if
} 39. Kb7 Qb4+ 40. Kc8 {and the d-pawn is decisive.}) 1-0

