1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Ne7 ({I was expecting the normal} 4... Nc6 5.
Nf3 Qb6 {, after which I wanted to play Milner-Barry's gambit idea of} 6. Bd3
cxd4 7. cxd4 Bd7 8. O-O Nxd4 9. Nxd4 Qxd4 10. Nc3 {, offering both centre
pawns for a powerful attack. I believe that this sacrifice is sound, and at
least it offers White excellent prospects in practical play. It is interesting
to know that both Botvinnik and Gligoric (against whom I reached the same
opening at Hastings, 1957-58, by transposition from the Sicilian with 2 c3 e6)
believe that Black's traditional development of the queen at b6 in the Advance
Variation of the French is incorrect.}) 5. Nf3 Nf5 ({If} 5... Nbc6 6. Na3 cxd4
7. cxd4 Nf5 8. Nc2 Qb6 9. Bd3 {and White consolidates his pawn centre.}) 6.
dxc5 ({Now} 6. Na3 {is not good, because of} cxd4 {and 7...Bxa3, breaking up
White's pawn front}) ({while if} 6. Bd3 cxd4 7. Bxf5 exf5 8. cxd4 Nc6 9. Nc3
Be6 {(Barden-Gligoric, Hastings 1957/58) Black has a solid position with two
bishops.}) 6... Bxc5 7. Bd3 Nc6 ({Afterwards I learnt that the World Champion 
(who was tired from his marathon with Gligoric) had seriously considered an
immediate 7...Nh4 for some while, turning it down for the positional reason
that he ought to develop further before moving a piece twice in the opening.
It was only after making his actual move that Botvinnik realised that} 7... Nh4
{loses a piece to} 8. Qa4+) 8. O-O Nh4 ({An interesting idea, threatening both
to win the e-pawn directly by ...Nxg3+ and to build up the pressure by ...Ng6
and ...f6.} 8... O-O {is also quite playable.}) 9. Re1 $2 ({A mistake which
indicates that White remembers too well the Nimzowitsch dictum that the
outpost square at e5 should be overprotected and underestimates the weakness
of his own f2.} 9. Bf4 {is also inferior after} Ng6 10. Bg3 f6 11. Bxg6+ hxg6
12. Qd3 Kf7 {, when Black can eventually force the exchange of the e5-pawn by .
..Bb6-c7 and ...Rh5 and then utilise his own central pawn mass.}) ({Correct is
} 9. Nbd2 {, so that if} O-O ({while if} 9... Ng6 10. Nb3 Bb6 11. Re1 {(now
there is no longer any danger to f2), after which the natural} Bc7 {fails to} (
{while if} 11... Bd7 12. Nbd4 Bc7 13. Nb5 Ncxe5 14. Nxc7+ Qxc7 15. Nxe5 Nxe5
16. Bf4 f6 17. Bxe5 fxe5 18. Qh5+ {, regaining the pawn with advantage.}) 12.
Bg5 Qd7 13. Nc5) 10. Nxh4 Qxh4 11. Nf3) {Black can, of course, treat the
position more quietly, but then White can maintain the e-pawn with reasonable
prospects of play on the black squares.} 9... Bd7 10. b4 ({This and the next
move form an over-impatient idea. White could still try} 10. Nbd2) 10... Bb6
11. b5 Nxf3+ 12. Qxf3 Ne7 ({Better than} 12... Na5 {, when} 13. Qg3 {is hard
to meet.}) 13. Ba3 $2 ({If} 13. Bg5 {, simply} O-O {, for} 14. Qh3 h6 15. Bxh6
gxh6 16. Qxh6 Nf5 17. Bxf5 {gives White insufficient attacking chances.}) ({
However, White could still maintain a fair game, despite the ragged queen's
wing pawns, by} 13. Nd2 {and if} Rc8 14. c4 Bd4 $2 15. Rb1) 13... Rc8 14. Qg3
Ng6 15. Nd2 ({White now sees that the previously planned} 15. c4 {fails to} Bd4
(15... dxc4 $2 16. Be4 {or even 16 Nd2!?, with a strong attack}) {, when White
has nothing for the exchange after} 16. Nd2 Bxa1 17. Rxa1 Qa5) ({The pawn
sacrifice is the only practical chance if White is to avoid a miserable move
like} 15. Bf1) 15... Rxc3 16. Bb4 Rc8 ({Post-mortem analysis established that
a still stronger idea is} 16... Rxd3 $1 17. Qxd3 Qh4 $1 (17... Bxf2+ {and 18...
Qh4+ is unclear}) 18. Ne4 {(the only chance: otherwise Black wins by ...Qxb4
or ...Bxf2+)} dxe4 19. Rxe4 Qxf2+ 20. Kh1 Nf4 21. Rxf4 Qxf4 22. Rd1 Qd4 $1 {
when White has to exchange off into a bishops of opposite colour ending a pawn
down, which should be won for Black. The World Champion saw most of this
variation (I have to admit that I did not consider it all during the game),
but rejected it on the safety-first ground that there is danger of
miscalculation in such sharp tactical lines. By safe play Black has the
advantage in any case, and "I prefer to avoid complications unless I am forced
into them," remarked Botvinnik.}) 17. Nf3 {Necessary to prevent the
simplifying ...Qh4.} Bc5 18. Bd2 O-O 19. Bg5 (19. h4 {was suggested as a
better attacking chance, but then Black continues} f6 $1 20. Bxg6 hxg6 21. Qxg6
Qe8 $1 {and White's attack is over.}) 19... f6 $1 ({A fine conception of a
sort often seen in Botvinnik's games with the French. In return for the
exchange, he obtains a concentration of centre pawns and active pieces which
makes White’s job of finding a constructive plan very difficult.
Positionally, the exchange sacrifice is even forced, for after} 19... Be7 20.
h4 $1 {White would have real attacking chances.}) 20. exf6 gxf6 21. Bh6 Kf7 $1
22. Bxf8 Qxf8 23. Rac1 $2 ({Here and at the next move, White loses time and
retains the wrong minor piece. Black’s Knight must be prevented from
occupying f4, but the best chance of doing so while restraining Black’s
central advance is} 23. Bxg6+ hxg6 24. Qg4 {As played, White’s bishop
becomes virtually useless.}) 23... e5 24. Nh4 Qh6 25. Nxg6 hxg6 26. Rc2 {
Black threatened ...Bxf2+.} b6 27. a4 e4 28. Bf1 f5 {The World Champion excels
in the art of utilising a central pawn phalanx, and hence­forward White is
steadily driven back and deprived of useful squares.} 29. Rec1 f4 30. Qb3 Be6
31. Rxc5 ({The return of the exchange is White's only chance to avoid being
gradually crushed; it is slightly better to preface it with} 31. a5) 31... bxc5
32. b6 c4 $2 ({In his eagerness to blot out the bishop completely, Black
advances too hastily.} 32... axb6 33. Qxb6 Qf8 {should win.}) 33. Qb4 $2 ({
Here I reached out my hand to grasp my b-pawn, intending to play} 33. b7 {
, noticing just as I did so that Black then wins immediately by} Rh8 $1 {
Mr Rhoden, the tournament controller, confirmed that I had not touched the
b-pawn, whereupon I hurriedly moved the queen, thus missing the chance of 33
bxa7!}) (33. bxa7 $1 {In view of the threat of 34 Qb7+ and queening, there are
only two plausible defences for Black.} cxb3 (33... Qf8 34. Qb7+ Kf6 35. Bxc4
$1 dxc4 36. Qxe4 {when White has two pawns for the piece and the black king is
dangerously exposed}) 34. Rxc8 Bxc8 35. a8=Q Be6 36. Qb7+ Kf6 37. Qxb3 {
, after which Black has to struggle for a draw. "If you go through my games at
the Hastings tournament of 1962 you may well expect my greatest error to have
occurred there. I missed a clear winning chance against the world champion,
Botvinnik..." (Barden, p239, "Chess Treasury of the Air," 1966).}) {Brief
tactical lapses like this and similar instances in the games with Robatsch and
Bisguier were the only blemishes in Botvinnik’s performance at Hastings.}
33... axb6 34. Qxb6 Qf8 {Now Black decisively regains his grip the position.}
35. Rb1 Qc5 36. Qb7+ Rc7 37. Qb8 Kg7 38. Rb6 Bf7 39. a5 c3 40. Qd8 Qe7 41. Qxe7
{(sealed) (Source: BCM, March 1962, p85)} ({White sealed the move} 41. Qxe7 {
, but resigned during the adjournment, as the ending is hopeless, e.g.} Rxe7
42. a6 Ra7 43. Rb7 c2) 0-1

