1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. Nc3 d6 4. d4 cxd4 5. Nxd4 g6 6. Be2 Bg7 7. O-O O-O 8.
Be3 Nc6 9. Qd2 Bd7 10. f4 Nxd4 11. Bxd4 b5 $16 (11... Qa5 $142 $5 {might be a
viable alternative:} 12. Rad1 Rfc8 $14) 12. e5 dxe5 13. fxe5 Ng4 14. Bf3 $14 b4
$16 ({In an uninspiring situation, Littlewood in typical fashion sacrifices
the exchange.} 14... Rc8 15. Rad1 b4 16. Ne4 Bxe5 17. h3 $15) 15. Bxa8 Qxa8 ({
If} 15... bxc3 $2 {then} 16. Bxc3 Qxa8 17. Qxd7) 16. Ne2 Bc6 (16... Qb8 17. h3
$1 {leaving no more doubts} Nxe5 18. Bc5 $18) 17. Qg5 h5 18. e6 $16 (18. Nf4
Bb5 19. Qxe7 Bxf1 20. Rxf1 $16) 18... Bxd4+ $2 $18 19. Nxd4 {With the decisive
threat exf7} Bxg2 20. Rxf7 $16 (20. exf7+ Rxf7 21. Qxg6+ Rg7 22. Qe6+ Kh8 $19)
20... Rxf7 21. exf7+ Kxf7 22. Qd2 $14 Qd5 23. Re1 $4 $19 {With this move White
loses his initiative.} Qd6 $1 {Doomsday. The mate threat is Qxh2.} 24. Qxg2
Qxd4+ 25. Kf1 e5 $4 $11 ({Allows the opponent back into the game.} 25... Ne3+
$142 {Black has the better game} 26. Rxe3 Qxe3 27. Qd5+ Kf6 28. Qc6+ Qe6 29.
Qf3+ (29. Qc7 Qf5+ 30. Kg2 Qg5+ 31. Kf3 Qd5+ 32. Ke2 Qe5+ 33. Qxe5+ Kxe5) 29...
Qf5 30. Kg2 Qxf3+ 31. Kxf3 Ke5) 26. Qe2 e4 $14 (26... Kf6 27. a3 Qd5 28. Kg1
Qc5+ 29. Kg2 bxa3 30. bxa3 $16) 27. h3 Nf6 28. b3 Qd6 29. Kg2 g5 $14 (29... Kg7
30. Qf2 Qd7 31. Rf1 $16) 30. Qe3 Kg6 31. Qxa7 g4 32. Qf2 Qd5 33. Qe3 gxh3+ 34.
Kxh3 Ng4 $2 $18 (34... Qc6 $142 $16) 35. Rg1 $4 $14 ({White loses the upper
hand.} 35. Qb6+ Kg5 36. Rg1 $14) 35... Qf5 $18 (35... Qe5 $5 {is worth looking
at} 36. Rg2 Kf5 $16) 36. Qb6+ Kh7 $2 $18 37. Qc7+ $16 (37. Qb7+ Kh6 38. Qa6+
Nf6+ 39. Kh2 $14) 37... Kh6 38. Qg3 $14 (38. Qd6+ $5 Nf6+ 39. Kh2 Qf2+ 40. Kh1
Qh4+ 41. Qh2 Qxh2+ 42. Kxh2 Ng4+ 43. Kh3 $16) 38... e3 39. Kg2 $15 (39. Rg2 $5
{is interesting} Qf6 40. Rg1 $11) 39... Qxc2+ 40. Kh1 $17 (40. Kh3 $5 {is
worthy of consideration} Nf2+ 41. Kh2 Ne4+ 42. Rg2 Nxg3 43. Rxc2 $11) 40...
Qe4+ 41. Rg2 Qb1+ $11 42. Rg1 Qf5 $11 43. Qd6+ Kg5 44. Qe7+ Kh6 45. Qd6+ Kg7
46. Qe7+ {Source: John Littlewood collection (Keith Brown)} 1/2-1/2

