1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 a6 6. Be2 e5 7. Nb3 Be7 8. O-O
O-O 9. Be3 ({One important aspect of White’s strategy in this variation is
that the f-pawn should only be advanced once Black has developed his queen’s
bishop. If Black plays ...Be6, then White wants to advance f4-f5 and thus gain
a useful tempo for a possible king’s-side attack and for the , struggle for
central squares. If Black places his bishop at b7, then White should safeguard
his e-pawn from attack by f3. This simple point is frequently disregarded even
in tournament practice. It seems particularly inferior for White to advance f4
when Black still has the chance to reply ...Bb7, e.g.} 9. f4 b5 10. a3 Bb7 11.
Bf3 Nbd7 12. g3 Rc8 13. Qe1 Nb6 14. Kh1 Nc4 15. Nd2 Qd7 {, with clear
advantage to Black (Yanofsky-Bolbochan, Stockholm 1962).}) 9... Qc7 10. a4 ({
A good alternative is the developing} 10. Qd2 {. White then has nothing to
fear from the advance of the b-pawn, e.g.} b5 ({If} 10... Be6 11. f4 exf4 12.
Rxf4 $1 Nbd7 13. Nd4 Ne5 14. Nf5 {(Geller- Polugaevsky, Moscow, 1961) gives
White a promising attack.}) 11. Nd5 Nxd5 12. exd5 Nd7 13. Rac1 Nb6 14. c4 Nxc4
15. Bxc4 bxc4 16. Na5 {, and White infiltrates on the white squares 
(Geller-Rothstein, Kiev 1959).}) 10... b6 ({He should not allow his queen’s
wing pawns to become fixed by a5, e.g.} 10... Nbd7 11. a5 $1 b5 12. axb6 Nxb6
13. Na5 Be6 14. Qd2 Rfd8 15. Bxb6 Qxb6 16. Nd5 Nxd5 17. exd5 {, with
considerable pressure for White due to the weakness of Black’s a6-pawn and
white squares.}) 11. Qd2 Be6 12. f4 ({More logical than the passive} 12. Rfd1
Rc8 13. Qe1 Qb7 14. Rd2 Nbd7 15. f3 {(see note to move 9 for the disadvantages
of this formation),} d5 $1 16. exd5 Nxd5 17. Nxd5 Bxd5 {, with advantage to
Black (Unzicker- Bronstein, Gothenburg, 1955).}) ({However, a still better
plan may be} 12. Nd5 Bxd5 13. exd5 {, followed even­tually by Bd3, f4, and
the utilisa­tion of the bishops for a king’s-side attack.}) 12... Rc8 {
Gligoric’s plan is the same as in Unzicker-Bronstein: the preparation of an
early ...d5.} 13. f5 Bc4 14. g4 Qb7 15. Bf3 ({This appeared a very promising
sacrifice at the time, since if Black accepts it directly he is driven into a
hopelessly passive situa­tion after 15 ...Bxf1 16 Rxf1, followed by Nd5, f6,
and most likely a winning king’s-side attack for White. However, the
Yugoslav grandmaster works out a method of taking the exchange while
maintaining active play for the black pieces. A direct} 15. g5 {fails against}
Nxe4 16. Nxe4 Bxe2 $1 17. Qxe2 Qxe4) 15... d5 $1 16. exd5 Bxf1 17. Rxf1 e4 $1 {
This second pawn sacrifice is the key to Black’s defence, and a move I had
under­estimated. Any quieter continuation per­mits White to advance g5 and
then sink his knight on e4: while as the game goes, White’s more dangerous
knight is exchanged off and Black has e5 available as a jumping-off square. In
such situations where the struggle for the initiative is acute, square control
and the activity of the pieces outweigh the formal value of pawns.} 18. Nxe4 ({
Unfortunately for White, the more natural} 18. Be2 {(to preserve an extra pair
of minor pieces) is unsatisfactory because of} Bb4 $1 {, when there is no good
defence for the d-pawn.}) 18... Nxe4 19. Bxe4 Nd7 20. Bf4 {Clearly, White must
contest the important central black squares.} Bc5+ 21. Kh1 $2 ({Now Black is
able to regroup his pieces and take control of the important centre files.
Better is} 21. Nxc5 Rxc5 22. Bf3 {, when White’s position is more solid in
the game and should be tenable.}) 21... Re8 22. Bf3 Rad8 23. d6 Qc8 24. Nxc5 $2
({Now this is a mistake, after which Black’s pieces burst into activity.
White may still be able to hold on with Gligoric’s suggestion of} 24. Re1 {
, since in the next few moves the combined influence of the black rooks helps
to give him the upper hand.}) 24... Qxc5 25. b4 {Overlooking the reply, but
otherwise Black cannot be prevented from playing ...Ne5 or ...Nf6-e4, with
great effect.} Qc4 26. Rb1 Nf6 27. b5 axb5 28. axb5 Ne4 29. Qb4 ({White has to
lose the d-pawn or the b-pawn, since both} 29. Qd3 {and 29 Qd1 permit a knight
fork.}) 29... Qxc2 30. Qb3 Qxb3 $2 ({This rather hasty exchange would have
made Black’s win difficult were it not that White had become very short of
time. After the better} 30... Qf2 {White would remain with a weak king’s
position as well as a material deficit.}) 31. Rxb3 Nxd6 32. Bc6 Re1+ 33. Kg2
Ne4 34. Bc7 Rd2+ 35. Kf3 $4 {Source: BCM, March 1962, p87} ({A move of a kind
which prompted grandmaster Bisguier to observe, very sensibly, that if one
does this sort of thing in time trouble one’s first consideration in a game
should be to keep on reasonable terms with the clock. After} 35. Kh3 {there is
no clear win for Black. White resigned without waiting for 35 Rf2 mate—a
decision prompted more by shock than any meanness towards the spectators.}) 0-1

