1. d4 d5 2. Nf3 Nc6 {Amusingly enough, Bisguier had played his 2nd move to
prevent the Albin Counter Gambit, and he grinned when he thought I was still
intent upon this design. However, on the spur of the moment I decided to pay a
tribute to the two Russians in the tournament by adopting the unusual
Tchigorin Defence.} 3. c4 Bg4 4. cxd5 ({A surprise for me. I had expected} 4.
Qa4 {, the move Alekhine had played in two games against Colle, and I was
planning to try out Bronstein’s idea of (after} Bxf3 5. exf3 e6 6. Nc3 {)}
Nge7 $1 {and 7...g6!}) 4... Bxf3 5. gxf3 ({If} 5. dxc6 Bxc6 6. Nc3 e6 7. e4 Bb4
8. f3 f5 $5 {, as played by Tchigorin against Pillsbury (St. Petersburg. 1895),
then} 9. Bc4 $1 fxe4 10. O-O $1 {(Karelov-Aronson, Leningrad, 1958) seems a
strong continuation for White, pointed out to me recently by the young
Sheffield player J. E. Scholes.}) 5... Qxd5 6. e3 e5 ({I am now committed to
giving up my other bishop for a knight, but this sets the stage for an
interesting struggle.} 6... e6 {, on the other hand, would be safer and would
probably lose.}) 7. Nc3 Bb4 8. Bd2 Bxc3 9. bxc3 exd4 10. cxd4 Nf6 ({A
fascinating position. White’s two bishops and three half-open files seem at
first sight to give him an overwhelming advantage, but matters are not so
simple. Black controls the centre and means to increase this control in the
next few moves, thereby restraining White’s weakened “huddle” of pawns.
The bishops need room; it is Black’s job to see that they don’t get it. In
my opinion, chances are even. Theory, however, gives White the preference,
although in the only games I have seen quoted supporting this view, Black 
(Fichtl and Dückstein) played the artificial} 10... Nge7 {, a move based on
fear.}) 11. Rb1 ({This move is indicative of White’s overconfidence in his
position. He would be better advised to see to the centre by} 11. Be2 O-O 12.
O-O Rad8 13. Bc3 {, threatening 14 e4. Even then, however, Black can maintain
the pressure by} Qe6 $1 {as} 14. e4 Nxd4 15. Bxd4 c5 {would be obviously bad
for White.}) 11... O-O 12. Be2 Rfe8 ({Strangely enough, as I discovered later,
this same position had occurred in a game Tarrasch-Schiffers (Nuremberg, 1896),
where Black here played} 12... Qxa2 {and the game continued} 13. Rxb7 Rab8 14.
Rb5 Rxb5 15. Bxb5 Rb8 $1 16. Bd3 $1 Rb2 17. Qc1 $1 g6 18. Bc4 {, with better
endgame prospects for White. It seems more logical to com­plete development
before “mixing it” in this way.}) 13. O-O ({If now} 13. Rxb7 {then} Nxd4 {
is good for Black.}) 13... Rad8 {Black has now completed his development and
is ready to gobble up White’s a-pawn, when 15 Rxb7 would lose to 13...Nxd4
16 exd4 Rxd4, etc.} 14. a4 Rd6 15. e4 $5 {Bisguier now realises that he is
drifting into a lost position, so he courageously takes the bull by the horns.
At the cost of a pawn, he hopes to exploit the endgame advantage of the two
bishops.} Qh5 $1 ({After} 15... Qxd4 16. Bf4 $1 Qxd1 17. Bxd1 Rd7 18. Rxb7 {
I prefer White’s game. Fortunately I have better.}) 16. d5 ({After the game,
Bisguier queried this move and suggested the line} 16. Be3 Nxd4 17. Bxd4 c5 18.
Rxb7 cxd4 19. Bc4 {, but after} Nd7 $1 20. Qb3 Ne5 {Black stands far better,
with mate in the offing (e.g.} 21. Rb8 $2 Nxf3+ 22. Qxf3 Rg6+ $1 {)}) 16... Nd4
17. Rxb7 {What else? White is lost now, whatever he plays, so he sets a little
trap.} Nxe4 $1 (17... Nxd5 $2 18. exd5 $1 Nxe2+ 19. Qxe2 Rxe2 20. Rb8+ {mates.}
) {White could now resign, but he sportingly decides to give the spectators
their money’s worth.} 18. Bf4 (18. fxe4 Nxe2+ 19. Kg2 Qg4+ {and mate next
move}) (18. Rb4 Rg6+ 19. Kh1 Qh3 20. Rg1 Nxf2#) 18... Rg6+ 19. Bg3 (19. Kh1 Qh3
20. Bg3 Rxg3 21. fxg3 Nxg3+ 22. Kg1 Ndxe2+ 23. Kf2 Qxh2+ 24. Ke1 Nc3+ {
followed by mate.}) 19... Nc3 20. Qd3 Ncxe2+ 21. Kh1 Qh3 22. Rfb1 Rxg3 23. fxg3
Nxg3+ {Undoubtedly this was my best game at Hastings. Source: BCM, February
1962, p59} 0-1

