[Event "Hastings Premier 1961/62 37th"]
[Site "Hastings, Sun Lounge"]
[Date "1962.01.04"]
[Round "8"]
[White "Littlewood, John Eric"]
[Black "Flohr, Salo"]
[Result "1/2-1/2"]
[ECO "B15"]
[Annotator "Flohr, Salo"]
[PlyCount "113"]
[EventDate "1961.12.27"]
[EventType "tourn"]
[EventRounds "9"]
[EventCountry "ENG"]
[SourceTitle "BritBase"]
[Source "John Saunders"]
[SourceDate "2023.01.13"]
[SourceVersion "1"]
[SourceVersionDate "2023.01.13"]
[SourceQuality "1"]

{In the penultimate round I faced Littlewood who plays a sharp, uncompromising
game. He always goes out for a win. I myself needed to win to catch up with
Gligoric. Against Littlewood it is easier to win or lose than to draw. I won
two pawns, and yet managed to draw a game characteristic of my uncertain form
these days. I must either play more chess or give up the game altogether!} 1.
e4 c6 2. Nf3 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 (3... d4 4. Ne2 c5 5. Ng3 Nc6 6. Bc4 e5 7. d3 h6 $5
{/\ g6, Bg7}) 4. Nxe4 Nf6 5. Nxf6+ exf6 6. d4 (6. Bc4 {is more precise, so as
to answer} Bd6 {with} 7. Qe2+) 6... Bd6 $14 (6... Bg4 7. Be2 $11) 7. g3 $15 {
There is not much for White in this positional treatment of this opening. Only
7 Bc4 or 7 Bd3 can trouble Black.} (7. Bd3 $142 $5 $14 {should be investigated
more closely}) (7. Be3 O-O 8. Bd3 Bg4 9. h3 Bh5 10. g4 Bg6 11. Nh4 Re8 12. Nxg6
hxg6 13. Qf3 Qa5+ 14. Kf1 Na6 15. c3 Nc7 16. h4 Qd5 17. Qxd5 Nxd5 18. Re1 Re7
19. Bd2 Rae8 20. Rxe7 Rxe7 21. c4 Nf4 22. Bxf4 Bxf4 23. h5 Rd7 24. hxg6 Rxd4
25. gxf7+ Kxf7 26. Ke2 Bh6 27. f3 Ke6 28. Rh5 Rd8 29. b3 Bg5 30. Rh1 Ke5 31. b4
Kd4 32. Rd1 Kc3 33. b5 Rh8 34. Be4 Rh2+ 35. Kf1 Rxa2 36. bxc6 bxc6 37. Bxc6 Be3
38. Rd7 a5 39. Rxg7 a4 40. Bd5 Bc5 41. Rc7 Kd4 42. Rc8 a3 43. f4 Rb2 {0-1
Muehlberg-Kholmov,R/Dresden 1956/EXT 99 (43)}) (7. Bd3 Bg4 (7... O-O 8. O-O Bg4
9. h3 Bh5 10. c3 Nd7 11. Re1 Re8 12. Rxe8+ Qxe8 13. Be3 Qe6 14. Qc2 Nf8 15. Nh4
Re8 16. Nf5 Bb8 17. Bd2 Qd7 18. Re1 Rxe1+ 19. Bxe1 Qe6 20. Qb1 Be2 21. Bc2 g6
22. Ng3 Bxg3 23. fxg3 f5 24. Bf2 Nd7 25. Qc1 Qxa2 {1-0 Chistiakov,A-Goldberg,G/
Sochi 1952/EXT 99 (25)}) 8. c3 O-O 9. Be3 Qa5 10. Qc2 Qh5 11. O-O-O Nd7 12. h3
Bxf3 13. gxf3 Nb6 14. c4 c5 15. d5 Nd7 16. f4 b5 17. cxb5 c4 18. Be2 Qh6 19.
Qf5 Nb6 20. Rhg1 Rac8 21. Rg4 c3 22. Bd4 Na4 23. b4 Kh8 24. Kc2 Rce8 25. Bd3
Rg8 26. Rdg1 Nb6 27. Kb3 Nc8 28. Qxh7+ Qxh7 29. Bxh7 {1-0 Bivshev,V-Goldenov,B/
Moscow 1952/URS-ch (29)}) (7. Be2 O-O 8. O-O Re8 9. Be3 Bg4 10. Re1 Nd7 11. Qd3
Qc7 12. g3 Nf8 13. Nd2 Qd7 14. d5 Bxe2 15. Rxe2 Ng6 16. dxc6 Qxc6 17. Qc4 Qd7
18. Nf3 Qf5 19. Nd4 Qh5 20. Qd3 Ne5 21. Qf5 Qxf5 22. Nxf5 Bf8 23. b3 Rad8 24.
Kg2 g6 25. Nh4 Ng4 26. Nf3 Bc5 27. Rae1 Nxe3+ 28. fxe3 f5 29. Kf2 Bb4 30. Rb1 {
1/2-1/2 Keres,P-Flohr,S/AVRO 1938}) 7... O-O 8. Bg2 Re8+ 9. Be3 Qb6 10. Qc1 {
Littlewood is no hoarder of pawns. That he thinks fit to protect the b-pawn is
proof enough that Black could gobble it up with profit after 10 0-0.} Qa6 {
Black inhibits 0-0} 11. c4 Bb4+ 12. Nd2 {White prepares the advance d5} Bxd2+
$11 {I never light-heartedly part with one of my two bishops; but in doing so
here I make a nuisance of myself. White cannot castle and on d2 his king is
awkwardly placed, as soon becomes evident. 13 Qxd2? Qxc4 is clearly bad for
him.} 13. Kxd2 Be6 14. Bf1 (14. b3 {looks more natural} b5 {would not be too
troublesome in reply.}) 14... Nd7 15. Qc3 b5 16. d5 {A double-edged move which
allows Black free use of the e5-square and an open d-file. 16 c5 instead would
weaken White's d5-square. But there seems little wrong with 16 b3. Perhaps
Littlewood did not like the possibility} (16. b3 bxc4 17. bxc4 Nb6) 16... Bf5 (
16... cxd5 17. cxb5 Qb7 18. Qd4 {was not so clear.}) 17. Be2 Ne5 18. Rhc1 Rad8
{Naturally better than winning a pawn by 18...Nxc4+ but at the cost of
allowing the drawish bishops-on-opposite-coloured-squares.} 19. cxb5 $19 (19.
Bc5 $5 bxc4 20. d6 $19) 19... cxb5 $17 ({The d-pawn can't run away. But I
could have quietly taken it at once with 19...Rxd5+.} 19... Rxd5+ $5 20. Ke1
cxb5 21. a4 $19) 20. Ke1 $19 (20. Qd4 Bd3 $17) 20... Qb7 $17 (20... Rxd5 $5 21.
a4 Bd3 22. axb5 Qxb5 23. Bd1 $19) 21. Qb3 $19 (21. Rd1 Rxd5 22. b4 Rdd8 23.
Rxd8 Qh1+ 24. Bf1 Rxd8 $19) 21... Rxd5 {At first sight it seems incredible
that the game should still last any length of time from here and that White
should survive. Such a position should be won in ten or fifteen moves.} 22. Rc7
$1 {Littlewood's cool yet spirited play demonstrates clearly that this young
Englishman can defend as well as attack.} Qxc7 23. Qxd5 a6 24. Rc1 $4 {
shortens the misery for White} (24. Qb3 $142 $19) 24... Qe7 (24... Nd3+ $142 $1
{makes sure everything is clear} 25. Qxd3 Qxc1+ 26. Bxc1 Bxd3 $19) 25. Rd1 Be6
$2 ({My old weakness reasserts itself; my appetite for pawns. Pawns are not
everything in chess! Already a pawn up, I should have simply concentrated on
attacking the white king. After} 25... Bg4 $1 {it would soon have been all up
for White. Apart from anything else, this move sets a neat trap; if White
replies} 26. Bc5 {Black can reply with} Qxc5 $1 27. Qb3 Nf3+ 28. Kf1 Bh3# {
That's how you ought to play chess, probing, probing all the time.}) 26. Qe4 ({
White's queen must not concede the long white-squared diagonal to Black.} 26.
Qd6 $5 Qb7 27. b3 Nf3+ 28. Bxf3 Qxf3 $19) 26... Bxa2 27. f4 Ng6 28. Qxe7 {
Threatening mate: Qxe8} Nxe7 29. Kf2 Nd5 {[#] Making every allowance for
White's two bishops you must concede that he is quite lost. The Flohr of 1935
would never have drawn this game. But it is 1962; at 53 I get tired during the
fifth hour of play, and things happen that I can hardly believe myself after
the game.} 30. Ra1 $19 (30. Bd4 h6 $19) 30... Bc4 $17 (30... Rxe3 31. Rxa2 Nb4
32. Kxe3 Nxa2 33. Bd3 $19) 31. Bxc4 bxc4 $1 {keeping the advantage} (31... Nxe3
{looks better here - Wood}) 32. Bd4 Re6 33. f5 Rc6 34. Ra5 Nc7 ({Better the
obvious} 34... Nb4) 35. Rc5 $19 {The only move otherwise comes 35...Nb5,
consolidating Black's position completely.} Rxc5 36. Bxc5 Nd5 {These moves up
to the 40th were made in time trouble and I certainly don't guarantee their
accuracy.} 37. Kf3 c3 38. b3 c2 39. Ba3 Nc3 40. Ke3 Na2 $17 ({The last move
before the time control: how often has it lost the game! I head for a pawn
endgame thinking it easily won. By} 40... Nb5 {followed by ...Kf8-e8-d7 I
could still win without undue trouble.}) 41. Kd2 c1=Q+ 42. Bxc1 Nxc1 43. Kxc1
Kf8 {Two hours study of the position during the adjournment had convinced me
that it could no longer be won with best play. My only hope was that
Littlewood might be too young to play like a Lasker...! Some have dubbed him
Littletal, on account of his style. Now he becomes Littlelasker! (We consider
Little Wood quite suitable - editor, Baruch H Wood)} 44. Kb2 (44. Kc2 g6 45.
Kc3 gxf5 $17) 44... Ke7 45. Ka3 Kd6 46. Kb4 g6 $1 ({This move took my opponent
by surprise. He went to sleep on the position for half an hour and... found
the one road to the draw. Clearly passive play such as} 46... Kc6 47. Ka5 Kb7
48. b4 {followed by b5 would lead to a totally drawn position in which Black's
king cannot make any headway.}) 47. Ka5 $1 (47. g4 $2 {for then Black would
win with} gxf5) 47... Kc5 48. Kxa6 $1 ({Again the only move. Against} 48. g4 {
Black would win by} gxf5 49. gxf5 Kd4 $1) 48... Kb4 49. Kb6 Kxb3 50. Kc5 Kc3
51. Kd6 Kd4 52. Ke7 $1 {White still seems to be in trouble but...} Ke5 53. Kxf7
Kxf5 54. Kg7 h5 $11 (54... g5 55. Kxh7 Kg4 56. Kg6 $11) 55. Kh6 {I had arrived
at this position with Botvinnik in our hotel room. My opponent found it over
the board. Bravo, Littlewood! Black is move-bound.} h4 (55... Kg4 56. Kxg6 f5
57. h4 {is a draw just the same.}) 56. gxh4 Kg4 57. Kxg6 {Source: CHESS, Vol.
27, no.401-2, 19 January 1962, p142} 1/2-1/2

