1. c4 g6 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. e4 d6 4. d4 Bg7 5. Be2 O-O 6. Bg5 c5 7. d5 e6 8. Nf3
exd5 9. Nxd5 {Capturing with the piece gives advantages to both White and
Black.} Re8 ({Now} 9... Qa5+ {would be answered by} 10. b4 {when the black
queen would have to retreat again.}) 10. Nd2 Nc6 11. O-O h6 12. Nxf6+ Bxf6 13.
Bxh6 Bxb2 14. Rb1 Bf6 15. f4 Nd4 16. Bg4 b6 17. Bxc8 Rxc8 18. Qg4 Bg7 19. Bxg7
Kxg7 20. f5 Qf6 21. Nf3 Rc7 22. Rbd1 Rce7 23. Ng5 Rh8 {Black is running short
of moves. He must maintain a rook on the second rank against White's fxg6.} 24.
h4 {Now the threat is 25 e5 and if 25...dxe5 26 Ne4, or if 25...Rxe5 26 fxg6.}
Kf8 25. fxg6 Qxg6 26. Rxd4 cxd4 27. Rxf7+ Rxf7 28. Ne6+ Qxe6 29. Qxe6 Rxh4 30.
Qxd6+ Kg7 ({A mistake in time trouble. Probably he didn't like the look of} 
30... Ke8 {[the text gives the mistake K-Q1]} 31. e5 {but} Rhf4 {would
threaten an immediate draw, so against 30...Ke8 [the illegal K-Q1 is again
given] White would eat up Black's remaining pawns beginning 31 Qb8+ Ke7 32
Qxa7+, etc.}) 31. Qg3+ Kf6 32. Qxh4+ {"and Black resigned three moves later
when the time control had been reached." Source: "Chess for the Love of It" by
A. R. B. Thomas (Routledge & Kegan Paul, 1973), p54} 1-0

